/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 1293 0 R
/E (Raissi, M., Perdikaris, P., & Karniadakis, G. E. \(2019\). Physics-informed neural networks: A deep learning framework for solving forward and inverse problems involving nonlinear partial differential equations. - This is the foundational paper that introduced the PINN framework. It's available in the Journal of Computational Physics and as an arXiv preprint.)
/Pg 30 0 R
/K [2]
>>
endobj
660 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 1294 0 R
/E (Jagtap, A. D., Kawaguchi, R., & Karniadakis, G. E. \(2020\). Extended Physics-Informed Neural Networks \(XPINNs\): A Generalized Space-Time Domain Decomposition Based Deep Learning Framework for Nonlinear Partial Differential Equations. - This paper introduced one of the first hybrid PINN architectures that uses domain decomposition to improve scalability and stability.)
/Pg 30 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
661 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 1295 0 R
/E (Wang, S., Teng, Y., & Perdikaris, P. \(2021\). Understanding and mitigating gradient pathology in physics-informed neural networks. - This is the key paper on adaptive loss weighting. It identifies gradient pathology as a major mode of failure in PINNs and proposes an adaptive algorithm to balance the loss terms. It was published in the SIAM Journal on Scientific Computing.)
/Pg 30 0 R
/K [4]
>>
endobj
662 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 1296 0 R
/E (Wang, S., Teng, Y., & Perdikaris, P. \(2020\). Understanding and mitigating gradient pathologies in physics-informed neural networks. arXiv preprint arXiv:2001.04536. - This is the arXiv preprint version of the previous paper, which you provided as a source. It details the analysis of gradient pathologies and the proposed solutions, including both the adaptive weighting algorithm and a new neural network architecture.)
/Pg 30 0 R
/K [5]
>>
endobj
663 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 1297 0 R
